  === Complete Solutions
  #sol-item(1, "C", dense: true, pad: 23.41pt)[
    The money value of everything produced in a year. Money holdings are a stock rather than a flow, exports are one component, and wages are only one of four factor incomes.
  ]

  #sol-item(2, "B", dense: true, pad: 23.41pt)[
    Inside the border, regardless of ownership. Production by citizens abroad belongs to GNP, and restricting the measure to one sector or one owner would make it something other than GDP.
  ]
  ]

  #sol-item(3, "D", dense: true, pad: 23.41pt)[
    Net factor income from abroad. Adding depreciation would not change the domestic-national distinction, and the tax and subsidy adjustments take market prices to factor cost.
  ]

  #sol-item(4, "A", dense: true, pad: 23.41pt)[
    GDP greater than GNP. Profits repatriated by foreign firms are earned inside the border but leave the country, and if they exceed what citizens earn abroad the domestic total is the larger.
  ]

  #sol-item(5, "B", dense: true, pad: 23.41pt)[
    Remove indirect taxes and add subsidies. That adjustment strips out the tax wedge between what buyers pay and what factors receive; depreciation is a different adjustment entirely.
  ]

  #sol-item(6, "C", dense: true, pad: 23.41pt)[
    Output, income and expenditure. B lists components of expenditure, C lists stages of economic activity, and D lists fiscal instruments.
  ]

  #sol-item(7, "A", dense: true, pad: 23.41pt)[
    Consumption plus investment plus government spending plus net exports. Adding exports and imports rather than net exports double-counts, and omitting net exports misses the foreign sector.
  ]

  #sol-item(8, "A", dense: true, pad: 23.41pt)[
    Wages, rent, interest and profit — one reward for each factor. The others are subdivisions of a single reward rather than the four-factor classification.
  ]

  #sol-item(9, "B", dense: true, pad: 23.41pt)[
    A pension is a transfer payment: money moves but nothing is produced. The other three are payments for current output or for newly produced capital.
  ]

  #sol-item(10, "B", dense: true, pad: 23.41pt)[
    It was counted when new. Only the dealer's margin, which is a fresh service, enters the accounts; counting the whole resale would double-count the same output.
  ]

  #sol-item(11, "D", dense: true, pad: 23.41pt)[
    Value added. Adding gross sales of every firm counts the flour once inside the bread and again at the mill; value added counts each firm's own contribution only.
  ]

  #sol-item(12, "C", dense: true, pad: 23.41pt)[
    ₦60,000 — the ₦100,000 of output less the ₦40,000 of intermediate purchases. The ₦40,000 already entered GDP when the flour was milled, and ₦140,000 adds the two figures together, which double-counts.
  ]

  #sol-item(13, "A", dense: true, pad: 23.41pt)[
    Factor services — land, labour, capital and enterprise — in return for rent, wages, interest and profit. Households buy goods rather than supply them, and tax is paid to the domestic government.
  ]

  #sol-item(14, "D", dense: true, pad: 23.41pt)[
    Government spending enters the flow from outside the household and firm sectors. Saving, taxation and imports all remove money from the flow, so they are withdrawals.
  ]

  #sol-item(15, "C", dense: true, pad: 23.41pt)[
    Imports pay foreign producers, so the money leaves the domestic flow. Investment, exports and government expenditure all add to it.
  ]

  #sol-item(16, "B", dense: true, pad: 23.41pt)[
    Withdrawals equal injections. The other identities either mix a leakage with an injection on the same side or omit one of the six flows altogether.
  ]

  #sol-item(17, "C", dense: true, pad: 23.41pt)[
    Rise. More money is entering the flow than leaving it, so total spending and hence output and income expand until the flows balance again.
  ]

  #sol-item(18, "A", dense: true, pad: 23.41pt)[
    Constant prices — the prices of a chosen base year — so that a change in the figure reflects a change in quantity rather than in prices.
  ]

  #sol-item(19, "D", dense: true, pad: 23.41pt)[
    ₦200 ÷ 1.25 = ₦160 billion. Multiplying instead of dividing gives ₦250 billion, and ₦175 billion is the ₦25 billion adjustment applied as a subtraction.
  ]

  #sol-item(20, "B", dense: true, pad: 23.41pt)[
    Unchanged: 1.20 ÷ 1.20 = 1.00. The entire nominal increase is price, so the volume of output is exactly where it was.
  ]

  #sol-item(21, "A", dense: true, pad: 23.41pt)[
    Nominal over real, times 100. The deflator is the price index implied by the accounts themselves, so nominal must sit on top.
  ]

  #sol-item(22, "A", dense: true, pad: 23.41pt)[
    The population. Dividing by the labour force gives income per worker, a productivity measure, and the two differ by the dependency ratio.
  ]

  #sol-item(23, "D", dense: true, pad: 23.41pt)[
    ₦36,000,000,000,000 ÷ 180,000,000 = ₦200,000. The others are each a slip of one or two places in the decimal point.
  ]

  #sol-item(24, "D", dense: true, pad: 23.41pt)[
    It is an average. A country can have a respectable figure and a population that has none of it, so distribution has to be measured separately.
  ]

  #sol-item(25, "C", dense: true, pad: 23.41pt)[
    Unchanged. A doubling of prices cancels a doubling of nominal income, so what the income can buy is the same as before.
  ]

  #sol-item(26, "D", dense: true, pad: 23.41pt)[
    The ratio of the change in national income to the change in the injection that caused it. The other options describe the average propensity to consume and to save, and a ratio of two changes that has no economic meaning.
  ]

  #sol-item(27, "A", dense: true, pad: 23.41pt)[
    k = 1 ÷ (1 − 0.75) = 1 ÷ 0.25 = 4. 1.33 is 1 ÷ 0.75, the reciprocal of the MPC rather than of the MPS.
  ]

  #sol-item(28, "D", dense: true, pad: 23.41pt)[
    Consume is larger, because a larger MPC means a smaller leak in each round. Larger saving, importing and taxing all reduce the multiplier.
  ]

  #sol-item(29, "A", dense: true, pad: 23.41pt)[
    k = 1 ÷ 0.2 = 5, and 5 × ₦5 billion = ₦25 billion. ₦4 billion is the first round of consumption, ₦5 billion forgets the multiplier, and ₦6.25 billion is 5 ÷ 0.8.
  ]

  #sol-item(30, "B", dense: true, pad: 23.41pt)[
    Income is respent. Each round of spending becomes somebody's income, and the fraction of it that is spent becomes income again, so the total exceeds the original injection.
  ]

  #sol-item(31, "C", dense: true, pad: 23.41pt)[
    Smaller. Each of the three is an additional leak from the flow, so more of every round of income stops circulating domestically.
  ]

  #sol-item(32, "C", dense: true, pad: 23.41pt)[
    No output is produced. The money is redistributed, not created, so counting it would inflate the total without any corresponding production.
  ]

  #sol-item(33, "A", dense: true, pad: 23.41pt)[
    Ignoring unpaid work. Cooking, cleaning and caring are output but are not transacted, so they escape the accounts and the measured total falls short of the real one.
  ]

  #sol-item(34, "B", dense: true, pad: 23.41pt)[
    Indirect taxes and subsidies: they are part of what buyers pay, so they sit inside the market-price figure and must be removed to reach factor cost.
  ]

  #sol-item(35, "C", dense: true, pad: 23.41pt)[
    Distribution. A high average with a small elite and a poor majority describes lives that the average does not represent, which is why the HDI and the Gini coefficient exist.
  ]

  #sol-item(36, "C", dense: true, pad: 23.41pt)[
    Government spending. Exports are the foreign sector's injection, saving is a household withdrawal, and investment is the firm sector's own injection.
  ]

  #sol-item(37, "A", dense: true, pad: 23.41pt)[
    400 + 120 + 150 + (90 − 110) = ₦650bn. ₦870bn adds exports and imports instead of subtracting, and ₦760bn omits the net export term.
  ]

  #sol-item(38, "C", dense: true, pad: 23.41pt)[
    Domestic means inside the border; national means by residents. The first option reverses the two, and the last two attach distinctions of price basis and of deflation that the two terms do not carry.
  ]

  #sol-item(39, "A", dense: true, pad: 23.41pt)[
    The informal sector. Much production is unrecorded, subsistence or paid in cash, so the official total misses activity that is genuinely happening.
  ]

  #sol-item(40, "C", dense: true, pad: 23.41pt)[
    The gap between the two rates — about 5 per cent. Adding them gives 11 per cent, and dividing 8 by 3 gives the 2.7 per cent that comes from treating the two rates as a ratio instead of a difference.
  ]

]

